[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Angular Input and Output Properties: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Angular's component-based architecture relies heavily on communication between components.  @Input and @Output decorators, along with EventEmitter, provide a robust mechanism for parent and child components to interact and share data. This tutorial will explore these concepts in detail."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"@Input: Passing Data from Parent to Child"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Input decorator allows a child component to receive data from its parent component.  It essentially defines a property on the child component that can be bound to a value in the parent's template."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Creating a Simple Child Component"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"import { Component, Input } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  template: '<p>Hello, {{ name }}!</p>'
})
export class ChildComponent {
  @Input() name: string;
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `ChildComponent` has an input property called `name`. The `@Input()` decorator makes this property available for binding in the parent component's template."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the Child Component in the Parent Template"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent',
  template: '<app-child [name]="parentName"></app-child>'
})
export class ParentComponent {
  parentName = 'World';
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the `ParentComponent` uses the `ChildComponent` in its template. The `[name]="parentName"` syntax binds the `name` input property of the `ChildComponent` to the `parentName` property of the `ParentComponent`.  The child component will display "Hello, World!"."},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"@Output and EventEmitter: Sending Data from Child to Parent"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Output decorator, in conjunction with EventEmitter, allows a child component to emit events that the parent component can listen to. This is the primary mechanism for child components to send data or signal events to their parent."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Child Component Emitting an Event"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"import { Component, Output, EventEmitter } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  template: '<button (click)="onClick()">Click Me</button>'
})
export class ChildComponent {
  @Output() buttonClicked = new EventEmitter<string>();

  onClick() {
    this.buttonClicked.emit('Button was clicked!');
  }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `ChildComponent` has an output property called `buttonClicked`, which is an instance of `EventEmitter`. When the button is clicked, the `onClick()` method is called, which emits an event with the string 'Button was clicked!'."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Parent Component Listening to the Event"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent',
  template: '<app-child (buttonClicked)="onButtonClicked($event)"></app-child><p>{{ message }}</p>'
})
export class ParentComponent {
  message: string;

  onButtonClicked(eventData: string) {
    this.message = eventData;
  }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ParentComponent` listens to the `buttonClicked` event emitted by the `ChildComponent` using the `(buttonClicked)="onButtonClicked($event)"` syntax. When the event is emitted, the `onButtonClicked()` method is called, and the event data (in this case, the string 'Button was clicked!') is passed as the `$event` argument. The parent component then updates its `message` property, which is displayed in the template."},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Passing Data with @Output"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `EventEmitter` can emit any type of data, not just strings. You can emit objects, arrays, or any other complex data structure."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Emitting an Object"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"import { Component, Output, EventEmitter } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  template: '<button (click)="onClick()">Click Me</button>'
})
export class ChildComponent {
  @Output() dataEmitted = new EventEmitter<{ name: string, value: number }>();

  onClick() {
    this.dataEmitted.emit({ name: 'Example', value: 123 });
  }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent',
  template: '<app-child (dataEmitted)="onDataEmitted($event)"></app-child><p>Name: {{ receivedData?.name }}, Value: {{ receivedData?.value }}</p>'
})
export class ParentComponent {
  receivedData: { name: string, value: number };

  onDataEmitted(data: { name: string, value: number }) {
    this.receivedData = data;
  }
}"},{"order":24,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use descriptive names for your @Input and @Output properties to improve code readability."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using interfaces or types to define the structure of data being passed between components, especially for complex objects."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid directly mutating @Input properties in the child component. If you need to modify the data, emit a new event with the updated value."},{"order":28,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"@Input and @Output decorators, along with EventEmitter, are fundamental concepts in Angular component communication. Understanding how to use them effectively is crucial for building well-structured and maintainable Angular applications. By following the examples and best practices outlined in this tutorial, you can confidently implement data sharing and event handling between your Angular components."}]
